  "question": {
    "text": "The rates of heat radiation from two patches of skin each of area&nbsp;<math><mi>A</mi></math>,&nbsp;on a patient&#39;s chest differ by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. If the patch of the lower temperature is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>300</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math> and emissivity of both the patches is assumed to be unity, then what is the approximate temperature (in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>) of the other patch? [Ignore all the higher-order terms in approximation]"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "301.5",
  "solution": {
    "text": "According to Stefan's law.<br /><br /><math><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>∆</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></math><br /><br /><math><mo>∴</mo><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>∆</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mfrac><mrow><mo>∆</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></math><br /><br />Now, <math><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mfrac><mrow><mo>∆</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>300</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi>&nbsp;</mi></math><br /><br /><math><mo>∴</mo><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mo>∆</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>300</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math><br /><br /><math><mo>∴</mo></math> Temperature of other patch = <math><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>∆</mo><mi>T</mi></math><br /><br />= 300 +1.5 = 301.5 K"
  }
